Dialogue: 3,0:30:39.05,0:30:44.80,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}THE WARD OF THE RED ROVER · MAY 1863
Dialogue: 0,0:31:06.86,0:31:09.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Her nurses nicknamed her Dragon Dix.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:09.25,0:31:12.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Thousands of women served as official Army nurses,
Dialogue: 0,0:31:12.57,0:31:15.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}and more than 20,000 worked in Union hospitals in
Dialogue: 0,0:31:15.83,0:31:16.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}some role.
Dialogue: 2,0:31:07.11,0:31:12.86,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1231 0 l 1231 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:31:07.11,0:31:12.86,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:31:07.11,0:31:12.86,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:31:07.11,0:31:12.86,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}STAFF AND NURSES · SEMINARY HOSPITAL, GEORGETOWN, 1865
Dialogue: 0,0:31:41.38,0:31:44.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}In the West, a widow from Illinois named Mary Ann
Dialogue: 0,0:31:44.27,0:31:47.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Bickerdyke cooked, cleaned and fought officers on
Dialogue: 0,0:31:47.16,0:31:48.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}behalf of the men.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:48.22,0:31:50.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}They called her Mother Bickerdyke.
Dialogue: 2,0:31:41.63,0:31:47.38,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 738 0 l 738 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:31:41.63,0:31:47.38,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:31:41.63,0:31:47.38,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}WOMAN'S WORK IN THE CIVIL WAR (1867)
Dialogue: 3,0:31:41.63,0:31:47.38,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}MARY ANN BICKERDYKE · ENGRAVING
Dialogue: 0,0:31:50.68,0:31:53.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}And in Washington, a poet came almost every day.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:53.53,0:31:56.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Walt Whitman brought paper, fruit, tobacco, and his time.
Dialogue: 2,0:31:50.93,0:31:56.68,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:31:50.93,0:31:56.68,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:31:50.93,0:31:56.68,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 9 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:31:50.93,0:31:56.68,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}WALT WHITMAN DURING THE WAR
Dialogue: 0,0:32:26.46,0:32:29.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}But many of those million cases had nothing to do
Dialogue: 0,0:32:29.47,0:32:30.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}with bullets.
Dialogue: 2,0:32:26.71,0:32:30.67,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:32:26.71,0:32:30.67,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:32:26.71,0:32:30.67,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:32:26.71,0:32:30.67,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}A WARD · HAREWOOD HOSPITAL, WASHINGTON, 1864
Dialogue: 0,0:32:50.36,0:32:53.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}For every Union soldier killed by the enemy, roughly
Dialogue: 0,0:32:53.82,0:32:55.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}two died of sickness.
Dialogue: 2,0:32:50.61,0:32:55.62,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:32:50.61,0:32:55.62,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:32:50.61,0:32:55.62,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:32:50.61,0:32:55.62,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}CONVALESCENT CAMP · ALEXANDRIA, VA.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:32.37,0:33:35.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Measles hit hardest in new recruits from farms, boys
Dialogue: 0,0:33:35.70,0:33:38.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}who had never met crowds or crowd diseases.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:38.46,0:33:40.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}In camp, they met everything at once.
Dialogue: 2,0:33:32.62,0:33:38.37,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:33:32.62,0:33:38.37,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:33:32.62,0:33:38.37,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:33:32.62,0:33:38.37,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}A RECRUITS' TENT CAMP · KENTUCKY
Dialogue: 0,0:33:41.28,0:33:43.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Pneumonia killed almost 20,000 more.
Dialogue: 2,0:33:41.53,0:33:44.09,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1167 0 l 1167 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:33:41.53,0:33:44.09,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:33:41.53,0:33:44.09,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:33:41.53,0:33:44.09,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}BARRACK WARDS · CONVALESCENT CAMP, ALEXANDRIA, 1864
Dialogue: 0,0:34:12.31,0:34:14.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Which brings us to the men who lived.
Dialogue: 2,0:34:12.56,0:34:14.43,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:34:12.56,0:34:14.43,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:34:12.56,0:34:14.43,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:34:12.56,0:34:14.43,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}CONVALESCENT SOLDIERS · BATON ROUGE
Dialogue: 0,0:34:21.06,0:34:24.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}If you survived all of it, the bullet, the field,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:24.34,0:34:26.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}the saw, the fever, you went home.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:26.62,0:34:30.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Often to a farm, often with one arm or one leg, to a
Dialogue: 0,0:34:30.10,0:34:33.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}country that had never seen so many wounded men.
Dialogue: 2,0:34:21.31,0:34:27.06,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1103 0 l 1103 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:34:21.31,0:34:27.06,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:34:21.31,0:34:27.06,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:34:21.31,0:34:27.06,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}CONVALESCENT CAMP HEADQUARTERS · ALEXANDRIA, VA.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:03.54,0:35:06.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Many veterans who had lost an arm refused the hook.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:06.79,0:35:08.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}They preferred an empty sleeve.
Dialogue: 2,0:35:03.79,0:35:09.16,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1167 0 l 1167 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:35:03.79,0:35:09.16,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:35:03.79,0:35:09.16,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:35:03.79,0:35:09.16,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}SOLDIERS AT A SANITARY COMMISSION HOME · WASHINGTON
Dialogue: 0,0:35:35.39,0:35:37.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}And Clara Barton kept working.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:37.31,0:35:40.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}After the war she ran an office that searched for
Dialogue: 0,0:35:40.46,0:35:43.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}missing soldiers, and helped reconnect more than
Dialogue: 0,0:35:43.54,0:35:45.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}20,000 of them with their families.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:45.78,0:35:48.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}In 1881, she founded the American Red Cross.
Dialogue: 2,0:35:35.64,0:35:41.39,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:35:35.64,0:35:41.39,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:35:35.64,0:35:41.39,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:35:35.64,0:35:41.39,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}CLARA BARTON
Dialogue: 0,0:35:49.06,0:35:50.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Nursing changed too.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:50.29,0:35:53.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}After the war, the nursing leader Mary Livermore
Dialogue: 0,0:35:53.25,0:35:55.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}praised how far it had come as a profession.
Dialogue: 2,0:35:49.31,0:35:55.06,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1338 0 l 1338 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:35:49.31,0:35:55.06,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:35:49.31,0:35:55.06,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:35:49.31,0:35:55.06,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}SANITARY COMMISSION STAFF AND NURSES · FREDERICKSBURG, 1864
Dialogue: 0,0:36:08.34,0:36:10.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Medicine kept something too.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:10.25,0:36:13.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}In 1862, Surgeon General Hammond founded the Army
Dialogue: 0,0:36:13.58,0:36:14.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Medical Museum.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:14.60,0:36:17.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Surgeons were ordered to collect and preserve
Dialogue: 0,0:36:17.66,0:36:18.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}specimens for it.
Dialogue: 2,0:36:08.59,0:36:14.34,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:36:08.59,0:36:14.34,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:36:08.59,0:36:14.34,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:36:08.59,0:36:14.34,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}DR. J. J. WOODWARD, ARMY MEDICAL MUSEUM
Dialogue: 0,0:36:30.98,0:36:33.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}It still exists, as the National Museum of Health
Dialogue: 0,0:36:33.83,0:36:34.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}and Medicine.
Dialogue: 2,0:36:31.23,0:36:34.98,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:36:31.23,0:36:34.98,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:36:31.23,0:36:34.98,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:36:31.23,0:36:34.98,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}DR. A. A. WOODHULL, ARMY MEDICAL DEPARTMENT
Dialogue: 0,0:36:35.03,0:36:38.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}The Surgeon General's office also built up a library
Dialogue: 0,0:36:38.27,0:36:40.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}of medical books during and after the war.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:40.89,0:36:44.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}It grew into what is now the National Library of Medicine.
Dialogue: 2,0:36:35.28,0:36:41.03,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1031 0 l 1031 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:36:35.28,0:36:41.03,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:36:35.28,0:36:41.03,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}PHOTO · PHOTOGRAPHIC HISTORY OF THE CIVIL WAR, VOL. 7 (1911)
Dialogue: 3,0:36:35.28,0:36:41.03,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}SURGEONS AND HOSPITAL STEWARDS IN WASHINGTON
Dialogue: 0,0:37:28.64,0:37:31.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Letterman resigned in December 1864.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:31.21,0:37:34.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}He died in 1872, and is buried at Arlington.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:34.36,0:37:37.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Today he is often called the father of modern
Dialogue: 0,0:37:37.57,0:37:39.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}battlefield medicine.
Dialogue: 2,0:37:28.89,0:37:34.64,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1510 0 l 1510 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:37:28.89,0:37:34.64,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:37:28.89,0:37:34.64,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}LETTERMAN, 1866 · VIA INTERNET ARCHIVE
Dialogue: 3,0:37:28.89,0:37:34.64,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}TITLE PAGE · MEDICAL RECOLLECTIONS OF THE ARMY OF THE POTOMAC, 1866
Dialogue: 0,0:38:09.57,0:38:11.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}His idea is still here.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:11.11,0:38:14.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}The chain he built, an aid station behind the line,
Dialogue: 0,0:38:14.51,0:38:17.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}a hospital behind that, and evacuation in between,
Dialogue: 0,0:38:17.85,0:38:20.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}remains the basis of military evacuation today.
Dialogue: 2,0:38:09.82,0:38:15.57,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1208 0 l 1208 132 l 0 132{\p0}
